{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfBS3EQmnVsrdzoYnhVmA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted **index 16 (Sb 0.66 / Zn 0.34)** as the best composition. Its EQE was 9.8×10⁻⁴ %, the highest of the three I measured, but every result was close to zero. That makes it a weak win, not a clear photoanode hit.\n\n| Index | Sb fraction | EQE (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 0.31 | 4.8×10⁻⁴ |\n| **16** | **0.66** | **9.8×10⁻⁴** |\n| 26 | 0.87 | −1.7×10⁻⁴ (no measurable response) |\n\n- **Why the values are so low:** the light is 2.1 eV, below the band gap of Zn–Sb oxides. So the whole line is probably at the noise floor, and the gap between 16 and 4 may not be real.\n- **Where 16 sits:** its composition matches ZnSb₂O₆, a known phase in this system, which is a reasonable reason for it to do best.\n- **XRD didn't help much:** I ran XRD on the same three points before measuring. All three share the same main peaks near Q ≈ 2.79, 4.55 and 5.33 Å⁻¹, so the patterns didn't point to a better region. Indices 16 and 26 had extra small peaks (near Q ≈ 1.90, 2.45 and 3.63) that index 4 lacked.\n\nThe library had 30 compositions and the merit budget was only three, so I spread them evenly across the line (Zn-rich, middle, Sb-rich). I spent all three at once rather than using each result to choose the next, so there was nothing left to test neighbours of index 16 (around 15 and 17). If the true best is a narrow peak between my three points, I would have missed it. Two of the five XRD scans went unused."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":386,"cache_read_input_tokens":8435,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":386},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id